PLACES=[
N('old-stone-church-rhinebeck','Old Stone Church (Evangelical Lutheran Church of St. Peter)','Rhinebeck','hud','worship','standing',41.96278,-73.88639,'site','view','drive','','1786',
  'The Palatine German Lutherans of the Rhinebeck flats organised in 1729 and built their first church on this spot, midway between the early settlements on the Albany Post Road, in 1716 (Wikipedia gives both years for the congregation\'s start and the first church). The present fieldstone church went up in 1786 when the congregation outgrew the first. A builder named Stephen McCarty remodelled the inside in the early 1820s, closing the side entrance and turning the pews to face the pulpit; a school building followed after 1832. As Lutheran churches opened in Rhinebeck and Red Hook villages the country congregation dwindled.',
  'A two-and-a-half-story fieldstone church with a four-stage tower at its southeast corner, high round-arched windows trimmed in brick, a one-story frame school, the churchyard and a frame parsonage to the north, on US 9 opposite Stone Church Road. Inside, according to the Register summary on Wikipedia, the pulpit is on the west wall and galleries of pews run round the other three sides. Interior not checked beyond that.',
  'The country church of Rhinebeck\'s Palatine Germans, still standing in fieldstone since 1786.',
  'Church property. Wikipedia says the area\'s Lutheran churches still hold occasional services here and a small church meets in it; service times were not checked, so see it from US 9 and the churchyard. The nearest mapped lot is about 250 m southwest on the Albany Post Road (US 9) and was not checked as public; whether the church has its own parking was not checked.',
  [W('Evangelical Lutheran Church of St. Peter')],
  conf='mixed',brick='some'),
N('staatsburgh-mills-mansion','Staatsburgh (Mills Mansion)','Staatsburg','hud','landmark','standing',41.85662,-73.92989,'exact','open','drive','','1832–1896',
  'Morgan Lewis, third governor of New York, bought the estate in 1792 and built a colonial house that burned in 1832, a fire said to have been set by unhappy tenant farmers. He and his wife Gertrude Livingston replaced it at once with a 25-room Greek Revival house. Their great-granddaughter Ruth Livingston Mills and her husband Ogden Mills had McKim, Mead and White rebuild it in 1895–96 as a Beaux-Arts mansion of 65 rooms, 14 bathrooms and 23 fireplaces. It is now a state historic site inside Ogden Mills and Ruth Livingston Mills State Park.',
  'The mansion, restored to how it looked about 1900, with interiors in French 17th- and 18th-century styles; NYS Parks says the rooms keep their original furniture, art and decoration. Admission to the house is by guided tour only. The grounds run down to the Hudson.',
  'A Greek Revival house of 1832 inside a Gilded Age mansion of 1896.',
  'NYS Office of Parks, Recreation and Historic Preservation. The grounds are open free from sunrise to sunset all year, with free parking (NYS Parks page, read November 18, 2026). House by guided tour only: regular tour $10 adults, $8 students and seniors, children 12 and under free; the house closes from October 31 to Thanksgiving for holiday decorating. Tour days and times were not given on that page; check with the site. Park in the lot beside the mansion.',
  [W('Staatsburgh State Historic Site'),STAATS],
  th=[41.85611,-73.92960],thn='Mansion parking lot, Old Post Road entrance',brick='no'),
N('hoyt-house-the-point','Hoyt House (The Point)','Staatsburg','hud','landmark','standing',41.85358,-73.93956,'exact','view','walk','','1855',
  'Calvert Vaux designed this three-story Gothic house of bluestone quarried on the site, and its 91 acres of picturesque grounds, in 1855 for Lydig Hoyt and his wife (TCLF names her Blanche Livingston, the Calvert Vaux Preservation Alliance Geraldine Livingston); he published it in his 1857 book Villas and Cottages. Robert Huntington redid the first-floor plasterwork about 1915. The state bought the estate from the Hoyt heirs (TCLF says 1962, Hudson Valley One 1963) to fill out Mills-Norrie State Park and meant to pull the house down for a swimming pool; it was spared but has stood empty since. The Calvert Vaux Preservation Alliance, formed in 2008, has raised money to stabilise it.',
  'From outside the chain-link fence: a compact Gothic stone house with tall gables, carved bargeboards, bay windows and an H carved into the east wall. In March 2020 Hudson Valley One found the windows boarded, the roof of the stone entrance portico half collapsed and the porch gone. A farm cottage was pulled down in 1963. The house is closed; the Alliance\'s site has a virtual tour of the interior.',
  'Calvert Vaux\'s bluestone villa, empty in the woods of a state park since the 1960s.',
  'State park land (Mills-Norrie State Park, NYS Parks). The house is boarded and fenced: see it from outside the fence only. It stands at the end of Mills Mansion Road, which OpenStreetMap marks as the blue trail, about 1 km west of the Staatsburgh mansion and its lot. Park hours were not checked beyond the Staatsburgh grounds (sunrise to sunset).',
  [L('The Cultural Landscape Foundation: Hoyt House','https://www.tclf.org/landscapes/hoyt-house'),L('Calvert Vaux Preservation Alliance: The Point','https://www.calvertvaux.org/the-point-an-historic-country-estate'),L('Hudson Valley One, March 2020','https://hudsonvalleyone.com/2020/03/26/preservationists-seek-to-restore-staatsburg-calvert-vaux-mansion-that-prefigured-central-park'),L('Hudson Valley Ruins: The Point','https://www.hudsonvalleyruins.org/rinaldi/PAGES/thepoint.htm')],
  conf='mixed',th=[41.85611,-73.92960],thn='Staatsburgh mansion lot',brick='no'),
N('vanderbilt-mansion-hyde-park','Vanderbilt Mansion','Hyde Park','hud','landmark','standing',41.79653,-73.94240,'exact','open','drive','','1764–1899',
  'One of the oldest Hudson River estates: Dr. John Bard bought land here in 1764 and built Red House, and his family held it until 1821. It later became one of the homes of Frederick William Vanderbilt and his wife Louise, for whom McKim, Mead and White built them a 54-room Beaux-Arts mansion between 1896 and 1899. It became a National Historic Site in 1940 and is run by the National Park Service.',
  'The mansion, with interiors blending European architectural salvage, antiques and period reproductions; entry is by guided tour (NPS). Lawns, formal gardens, woods and outbuildings on a bluff over the river, and Bard Rock on the shore below.',
  'A Gilded Age mansion on one of the oldest estates on the river.',
  'National Park Service. The mansion is seen by guided tour; the entrance pass is $15 a person (under 16 free) or an annual or America the Beautiful pass (NPS fees page, November 18, 2026). The main lot is beside the Pavilion Visitor Center and is open daily during operating hours; Bard Rock is open to walkers daily in park hours. The hours themselves were not read (the NPS page keeps them in closed panels).',
  [W('Vanderbilt Mansion National Historic Site'),L('NPS: hours','https://www.nps.gov/vama/planyourvisit/hours.htm'),L('NPS: fees','https://www.nps.gov/vama/planyourvisit/fees.htm')],
  th=[41.79852,-73.94084],thn='Visitor center lot, Vanderbilt Park Road',brick='no'),
N('wilderstein','Wilderstein','Rhinebeck','hud','landmark','standing',41.894,-73.94217,'exact','open','walk','','1852–1888',
  'Thomas Suckley bought 32 acres of river front in 1852 and built an Italianate villa here in 1852–53. His son Robert Bowne Suckley enlarged and remodelled it in 1888 into the Queen Anne house with its tall tower, with interiors by Joseph Burr Tiffany, while Calvert Vaux laid out drives, trails and outbuildings. Three generations of Suckleys lived here; the last, Daisy Suckley, a cousin of Franklin D. Roosevelt, died in the house in 1991. She founded Wilderstein Preservation and opened the house to visitors at Christmas 1984.',
  'Ground-floor rooms of 1888 in the Aesthetic Movement style, with mahogany, leather, stained glass and linen (Wikipedia); the house is seen on tours. About three miles of Vaux\'s carriage drives and trails, taking about an hour. HMdb markers on the trails say the foundations of the boathouse (burned 1937), its deep-water landing, the foundations of the Ellerslie dockmaster\'s house and the old stone road to Lewis Landing can be seen.',
  'Daisy Suckley\'s Queen Anne house and the ruins on its riverbank trails.',
  'Wilderstein Preservation (nonprofit). The grounds are open daily 9 a.m. to 4 p.m. and walking them is free; when the house is not open for tours, park for the trails off Morton Road by the gate lodge (wilderstein.org, November 18, 2026). Mansion tour days and prices were not checked.',
  [W('Wilderstein'),L('Wilderstein: grounds and trails','https://wilderstein.org/grounds-trails'),L('HMdb: Wilderstein\'s Boathouse','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=35437')],
  th=[41.89457,-73.94452],thn='Morton Road by the gate lodge',brick='?'),
N('kip-beekman-heermance-ruin','Kip–Beekman–Heermance house ruin (Kipsbergen)','Rhinecliff','hud','landmark','ruins',41.92611,-73.94583,'site','view','drive','','1700–1910',
  'Hendrick Kip, one of the patentees, built a stone house here about 1700; Colonel Henry Beekman Jr. and later his grandson Colonel Henry Brockholst Livingston lived in it. It burned in the early 20th century (the Rhinebeck historical society\'s photographs are labelled before and after 1910). Franklin Roosevelt modelled the Rhinebeck post office on it, and stones from the ruin were used to build that post office in 1940. The site was listed on the National Register in 1989.',
  'A historic marker on Rhinecliff Road (County Route 85). The town\'s 2014 inventory sheet says portions of the foundation, chimney and wall remain; a 2013 blog post shows one standing section of stone wall in woods, and the Rhinebeck historical society says the north-east corner wall was left standing.',
  'The 1700 stone house whose stones went into Roosevelt\'s Rhinebeck post office.',
  'Ownership of the ruin was not found: look from the marker on Rhinecliff Road and do not go onto the ground without the owner\'s leave. No parking is mapped; stopping on the road shoulder was not checked.',
  [W('Kip-Beekman-Heermance Site'),L('Town of Rhinebeck inventory sheet, 2014','https://rhinebeckhistory.org/Rhinebeck_TownMultiResourceDistrict_Data_Pages_Images_2014_06/Kip-Beekman-Heermance%20Archeological%20Site-%20Rhinecliff%20Road.pdf'),L('Historic Rhinebeck: Beekman house','https://historicrhinebeck.org/beekman-house/'),L('Mission: History blog, 2013','https://mission-history.blogspot.com/2013/08/ruins-in-woods.html')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('rhinebeck-post-office','Rhinebeck Post Office','Rhinebeck','hud','civic','standing',41.92639,-73.91333,'site','open','drive','','1940',
  'A New Deal post office that Franklin Roosevelt, from nearby Hyde Park, took a hand in designing. He chose the ruined Kip–Beekman house of 1700 (Kipsbergen) as its model, and stones from that ruin make up most of the front wall; he spoke at the dedication. Olin Dows, head of the Treasury Relief Art Project, painted murals of Rhinebeck\'s history inside.',
  'A one-and-a-half-story fieldstone building with a steep jerkin roof, a wooden porch on square piers over a bluestone floor, and bluestone cornerstones at each end, one saying the building copies the 1700 Beekman house and reuses its stones. Inside, the lobby has murals above the wainscot (one shows the groundbreaking) and two cases holding other remnants of the Beekman house (Wikipedia).',
  'Roosevelt\'s post office, built from the stones of a 1700 house.',
  'A working United States Post Office: the lobby is open in post office hours, which were not checked. It is on Mill Street (US 9) in the village; street and village parking.',
  [W('United States Post Office (Rhinebeck, New York)')],
  brick='no'),
N('beekman-arms-rhinebeck','Beekman Arms','Rhinebeck','hud','hotel','standing',41.9266,-73.913,'exact','open','drive','','1766',
  'William Traphagen opened a travellers\' inn in Rhinebeck in 1704; his grandson Arent Traphagen moved the tavern in 1766 to this corner, where the King\'s Highway (later the Post Road) crossed the Sepasco Trail. As the Bogardus, Potter\'s and Jacques\' tavern it saw the 4th Continental regiment drill on its lot in 1775 and served Morgan Lewis and Martin Van Buren. The inn claims to be the oldest continuously operated hotel in America (its own claim, as Wikipedia reports it).',
  'An inn and restaurant on the main crossroads of the village, run with the Delamater Inn (the 1844 Henry Delamater House) round the corner. What of the 18th-century fabric survives inside was not checked.',
  'A tavern on this corner since 1766, still taking guests.',
  'A private hotel and restaurant, open to guests and diners; hours not checked. At 6387 Mill Street (US 9) in the Rhinebeck Village Historic District; street and village parking.',
  [W('Beekman Arms Inn')],
  brick='?'),
N('neher-elseffer-house','Neher–Elseffer House','Rhinebeck','hud','landmark','standing',41.95889,-73.89167,'site','view','drive','','1746',
  'A frame farmhouse built in 1746, one of the few pre-Revolutionary frame houses left round Rhinebeck. It took its present look in renovations about 1800, and the inside was altered in the 1830s. The town owns it; a fire in 2000 destroyed the front, and it was listed on the National Register in 2003.',
  'A one-and-a-half-story, five-bay house with a saltbox-profile roof of seamed metal on a stone foundation, with a small flat-roofed south wing, on a wooded lot on the east side of US 9. The front is modern plywood put on after the fire. Wikipedia says the rooms are empty and used for storage and the fire-damaged north fireplace is boarded up.',
  'A 1746 frame farmhouse, burned in 2000 and kept by the town.',
  'Town of Rhinebeck property used for storage; not open. See it from US 9 north of the junction with NY 9G. No parking is mapped; where to stop was not checked.',
  [W('Neher–Elseffer House')],
  brick='no'),
N('elmendorph-inn','Elmendorph Inn','Red Hook','hud','hotel','standing',41.99722,-73.87389,'exact','open','drive','','1750s',
  'A mid-18th-century inn for travellers on the Albany Post Road, which made Red Hook a regular stop from 1785; for most of that century the hamlet was little more than the inn and a few houses. It appears as Elmendorph\'s Inn on a 1797 map. The Red Hook Town Board met here from 1815 until 1842. After a restoration it is the community centre and exhibit house of Historic Red Hook.',
  'A two-story, nine-bay clapboard building with a modified gambrel roof and four brick chimneys. Inside, much of the early 19th-century Federal finish remains: wooden doors, lath and plaster walls, chair rails and exposed ceiling beams, with bricked-up old fireplaces showing how it was enlarged (Wikipedia). Historic Red Hook runs a free drop-in tavern exhibit during its open hours.',
  'Red Hook\'s 18th-century Post Road tavern, open again as an exhibit.',
  'Historic Red Hook (nonprofit). Free admission, drop in during the society\'s posted open hours (historicredhook.org/visit, November 18, 2026; the hours themselves are given event by event and were not checked). At 7562 North Broadway; free parking behind the inn on Cherry Street.',
  [W('Elmendorph Inn'),L('Historic Red Hook: visit','https://www.historicredhook.org/visit')],
  th=[41.99763,-73.87274],thn='Lot behind the inn, Cherry Street',brick='some'),
N('montgomery-place','Montgomery Place','Annandale-on-Hudson','hud','landmark','standing',42.01444,-73.91889,'exact','open','walk','','1805',
  'Janet Livingston Montgomery, widow of General Richard Montgomery, built the house and moved in when it was finished in 1805, naming it Chateau de Montgomery. Alexander Jackson Davis designed for later Livingston owners, among them a cottage of 1849 and outbuildings built after 1860. The family sold it to Sleepy Hollow Restorations (later Historic Hudson Valley), and Bard College bought it in 2016; it is now Bard\'s Montgomery Place Campus and a National Historic Landmark.',
  'A Federal house of stucco over rubble stone with two frame wings and brick chimneys; Wikipedia says much of the original woodwork remains inside. Five outbuildings round a garden, an 1861 barn and an octagonal stone reservoir building. Four trails (Saw Kill, South Woods, Morning Walk, West Lawn) circle the house and gardens; the South Woods Trail goes through old-growth forest to the Hudson and the Saw Kill Trail to the creek.',
  'A Livingston house of 1805 with gardens and trails to the river.',
  'Bard College. The grounds are open daily, sunrise to sunset; park at the Visitors Center (a five- to ten-minute walk to the house). House tours in 2026 were on Saturdays in July and August at 10:30 a.m., $15 (bard.edu/montgomeryplace, November 18, 2026). The orchard and farm stand by the entrance are leased to Montgomery Place Orchards.',
  [W('Montgomery Place'),L('Bard College: visit Montgomery Place','https://www.bard.edu/montgomeryplace/visit/')],
  brick='no'),
N('watts-de-peyster-hall-tivoli','Watts de Peyster Fireman\'s Hall','Tivoli','hud','civic','standing',42.05889,-73.91139,'exact','open','drive','','1898',
  'John Watts de Peyster, village president and an authority on firefighting, had local architect Michael O\'Connor build this firehouse in 1898 and leased it to the village. In a tax dispute in 1900 he shut the village board out of it. His descendants bought out the lease in 1921 and gave the building to the village. It was the firehouse until 1986 and was restored in 1994.',
  'A three-story city-style "storefront" firehouse faced in brick laid in English bond, with stone bands at the floors, a hipped slate roof and a round turreted tower at the southwest corner; a band of stone carved with oak leaves, a cornerstone naming the architect and builder, and a marble plaque to de Peyster. Interior not checked.',
  'A city firehouse with a turret, given to a village of a few hundred people.',
  'Village of Tivoli. Since 1986 it has held the village offices and the Tivoli Free Library, and, according to Wikipedia, a Tivoli Bays visitor centre of the Hudson River estuarine research reserve; their opening hours were not checked. On Broadway in the village; street parking.',
  [W('Watts De Peyster Fireman\'s Hall')],
  brick='yes'),
N('clermont-livingston-manor','Clermont','Germantown','hud','landmark','standing',42.08589,-73.91918,'exact','open','drive','','c. 1740–1782',
  'Robert Livingston was willed this southwest corner of Livingston Manor in 1728 and built the first house about 1740. In October 1777 a British raiding party under Major General John Vaughan burned it because the family backed the Revolution; Margaret Beekman Livingston rebuilt it between 1779 and 1782. Her son Chancellor Robert R. Livingston, of the committee that drafted the Declaration of Independence, lived here. Seven generations of Livingstons held it until Alice Livingston gave it to the state in 1962.',
  'The house, shown as it was about 1930 when Alice Livingston and her daughters lived there, with a staff of servants; NYS Parks also tells the story of the enslaved people and tenant farmers of the estate. Gardens laid out by Alice Livingston, locust groves, and six carriage and farm trails over 500 acres (HMdb visitor-centre marker), with views to the Catskills.',
  'The Livingstons\' home for two centuries, rebuilt after the British burned it.',
  'NYS Office of Parks, Recreation and Historic Preservation. The mansion is open for tours all year, Thursday to Sunday, 11 a.m. to 4 p.m.; gardens and grounds 8:30 a.m. to sunset all year; a vehicle fee is collected at pay stations in the lot on weekends and Monday holidays, April 1 to October 31 (NYS Parks page, November 18, 2026). Tour prices on the page list $8 for seniors and students.',
  [W('Clermont State Historic Site'),CLER,L('HMdb: Welcome to Clermont','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=303761')],
  th=[42.08387,-73.91876],thn='Clermont parking lot',brick='no'),
N('arryl-house-ruins','Arryl House ruins','Germantown','hud','landmark','ruins',42.08202,-73.91930,'site','public','walk','','1793–1909',
  'Chancellor Robert R. Livingston finished this house in 1793 to replace Belvedere, burned by the British in 1777; first called New Clermont, it became Arryl House, a spelling of his initials R.R.L. It was an H-shaped house with piazzas on two sides, and the Chancellor used it for his scientific work. Sold out of the family in the late 1800s and bought back by John Henry Livingston in 1906, it was destroyed in November 1909 by a fire started by wind-blown embers from burning leaves.',
  'The stabilised ruins, which Alice Livingston worked into her gardens after the fire and which are kept as a memorial to the Chancellor; an HMdb-listed interpretive marker with old photographs stands at them. How much of the walls stands was not checked.',
  'The Chancellor\'s house, burned in 1909 and kept as a garden ruin.',
  'Clermont State Historic Site (NYS Parks): grounds 8:30 a.m. to sunset all year; vehicle fee in season as for Clermont. Park in the Clermont lot and walk south.',
  [L('HMdb: Arryl House','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=303786'),CLER,W('Clermont State Historic Site')],
  th=[42.08387,-73.91876],thn='Clermont parking lot',brick='?'),
]
